msgid "4:3, stretched"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "16:9, stretched"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "4:3, cropped"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "16:9, cropped"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Aspect ratio"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Crop position"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "-100: left or top, 0: the middle, 100: right or bottom."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Bend the cut parts onto the edges"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Undistorted middle"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Curvature radius"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The whole picture stays visible: the middle (in % of the frame) as cropped, the parts the crop would cut squeezed onto the edges as on a cylinder seen from the front. A small radius bends hard at the edge, a large one squeezes evenly."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Original: the picture's own shape, 4:3 (16:9 in widescreen)."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Black bars: the frame's shape, with the whole picture inside at its own shape and black bars where the shapes differ. Stretched: the picture fills the frame, deformed. Cropped: the picture fills the frame at its own shape, cut on the sides (or above and below); the crop position moves the part kept. The display mode then fits the frame to the window or the screen, or stretches it."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Window, square pixels"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Window, stretched"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Full screen"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Full screen, stretched"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Borderless fullscreen window"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Borderless fullscreen window, stretched"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Display mode"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "On a CRT driven at a super resolution (1920x240): its real shape as the aspect ratio (4:3 or 16:9, black bars) and a stretched mode. The frame then covers the screen, whatever the shape of its pixels."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Window size"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The window can also be resized with the mouse."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Whole multiples only (integer scaling)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The picture at 1x, 2x, 3x... its size only, for even pixels, with larger black bars. Not with a stretched or cropped picture, nor a stretched display mode."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Smooth"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Sharp pixels"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Scaling filter"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Sharp pixels: square and crisp, but uneven unless the picture is enlarged a whole number of times. Smooth: down to 240 rows (a CRT), each one the mean of two, where Sharp pixels drops one in two (thin lines, small texts)."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Off (black)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The picture's edges, blurred"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The game's bezel picture"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Bezel"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Around the picture where the window is wider than its frame: black, the picture's own edges blurred and dimmed, or the game's bezel picture with the game in its screen. The pictures are Batocera's: artwork\\bezels\\<set>.png beside the program and its <set>.info (where the screen is; else the picture's see-through middle), a clone taking its parent's, else default.png; black without any."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Vertical sync"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Limit the frame rate"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Target frame rate"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Show the frame rate (F11)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Show the frame number in the title bar"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Hide the mouse pointer"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Capture the mouse with a click"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "A click in the game's window keeps the mouse in it, hidden; Tab, Alt, the Windows key or another window let it go"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Draw on a thread of its own"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Each frame drawn while the next one is emulated: faster when the drawing slows the game, but the picture comes a frame later. Takes effect at the game's next start."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Sort the translucent polygons at each pixel"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Blended from the farthest at each pixel, as the board does: right where they cross. Needs OpenGL 4.3 and no MSAA; off, each mesh is sorted as a whole, lighter for a weak graphics card."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Processor (CPU)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Graphics card (GPU)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "NAOMI 2 lighting"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The NAOMI 2's lights (Club Kart, Initial D, Virtua Fighter 4...): on the processor's cores, as the board lights, or by the graphics card's shaders, the processor free for the rest (OpenGL 4.3; a colour a step off here and there)."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Widescreen 3D (16:9)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "4:3, in the middle"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Stretched to 16:9"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "2D in widescreen"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The 3D seen wider, at 16:9, its middle as the game draws it; the 2D (texts, logos) at 4:3 in the middle, or stretched for a game that needs it. The game may leave objects out at the sides, made for 4:3. Applied when the settings close."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Draw distance"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The 3D scenery's detailed models and its fog that much farther; what the game itself leaves out is not drawn. x1.0: as the game draws it."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Off"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Antialiasing (MSAA)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The 3D's edges smoothed by several samples a pixel. The textures' own edges (fences, leaves) stay as they are."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Edge smoothing (FXAA)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The 3D's edges smoothed once drawn: cheaper than MSAA, a little blurrier; the 2D left alone."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Anisotropic filtering"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The textures seen at a slant (roads, floors, walls) kept sharp into the distance."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Texture upscaling"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The textures enlarged, the edges of their flat colours (texts, signs, markings) kept sharp; the photographic ones change little. More memory, and the textures take longer to load."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Bloom"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "A glow around the 3D's bright parts (lights, sky, explosions). 0: none."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "CRT preset"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "None"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "A CRT drawn over the whole picture by a shader preset (data\\crt beside the program): Lola Retro's, by Zoki. sega-monitor-29 and sega-projector-50: the cabinets' screens; pc-monitor-vga: PC monitors, from good to extreme (-flat: a flat tube). The tube stays 4:3; a crop's bent edges are not drawn. Needs OpenGL 3.3."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "CRT filter"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Not with a CRT preset, which draws its own scanlines and mask."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "A CRT's look on a flat screen: its scanlines and its phosphors' stripes, over the whole picture. From 960 rows up (a window of x2 or more, full screen); none on a CRT itself. 0: none."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The sound card's own"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Mono"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Stereo"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "2.1 (stereo and subwoofer)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Quad (front and rear)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Speakers"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The game's sound is its cabinet's: the front speakers', and the rear's when it has some. The rear goes to the rear speakers (quad, 5.1, 7.1), or into the front without; the subwoofer gets all of the sound, low-passed; the center speaker none. The sound card's own: the speakers Windows has set for it."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Front / rear balance"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Front at %d %%, rear at %d %%; 50 for both at their full level."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Volume"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The changes take effect as soon as the game goes on."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The board's setting"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "On"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Attract sound"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The sound of the game's attract, while nobody plays. Written to the game's EEPROM at its start; the board's setting: as its BIOS menu set it (ADVERTISE SOUND, off on a new board)."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Latency"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The sound kept ahead in the output: less, the sound closer to the picture, but gaps when a frame comes late; more, safer on a slow PC. The output opens again with it."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Optimized"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Interpreted"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Sound emulation"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Optimized keeps the sound chips' channel registers and effects program decoded until the game writes them; interpreted decodes them at each sample, slower, as the reference. The same sound either way; the change takes effect at once."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The board's own"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Japan"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "USA"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Export"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Australia"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Korea"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Region"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The board's country, which the games read for their language and texts (Brave Firefighters in English outside Japan). Written to the game's EEPROM at its start; the board's own: the country its BIOS menu set."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Dynamic recompiler (dynarec)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Interpreter"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "SH-4 processors"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The dynarec runs their code translated to x86-64; the interpreter, one instruction at a time, as the debugger does. Both give the same machine: the change takes effect at the next frame."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Regions, one after the other"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Page table"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Fastmem"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Fastmem takes much more memory: 512 MB of address space reserved for each SH-4, their RAM and ROM mapped there a second time. For no significant gain in emulation speed."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Memory access"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "How the CPUs find their RAM and ROM: the page table at once, the regions searched one after the other (the first way), fastmem at their own addresses in the PC's address space. The same machine either way: the change takes effect at the next frame."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "SH-4 clock"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The SH-4s' clock: 200 MHz is the board's. Their timers, the MIE and the sound keep real time at any clock; the faster, the more of the PC the waits the games poll take (at 200 MHz, the games' boots run below 60 frames a second on this PC). 150 MHz by default: at 100 MHz some games show graphical glitches; below, Air Trix's skater loses his body."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "NAOMI SH-4 clock"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The SH-4 clock of the NAOMI, NAOMI 2 and Atomiswave: their games unpack their pictures by the time each frame leaves, so 200 MHz, the board's, by default: slower, pictures show before they are complete."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "I/O board running its own program"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The JVS I/O board's processor runs its program, as on the cabinet, instead of its simulation: 315-6215 (MAME's jvs13551 set), epr-21868 for NASCAR Arcade (the 18wheelr set); without the file, the simulation. Test and Service then go through that board, as a cabinet's."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Security chips"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The ROM board's X76F100 and the main board's 93C46 answer from their images (x76f100.ic85 and 93c46.ic115, in MAME's newer hikaru set), the BIOS checking them instead of skipping the check; NASCAR Arcade's protection answers as its Actel chip."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Network outputs (MAME protocol)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The cabinet's lamps and NASCAR Arcade's drive board commands, sent as MAME's network output sends them, as Supermodel, Flycast and sm2-emu do: for MAMEHooker, BackForceFeeder, DOFLinx and the like. Taken at once."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "TCP port"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The server's port, on all of this PC's addresses: 8000 as in MAME; another one for a second instance."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Announcement port (UDP)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "When a game starts, its name and the TCP port go once to this port on this PC, as Supermodel sends them (8001, where BackForceFeeder listens). 0: none."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Network outputs off."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "The server listens while a game runs."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Not listening: %s"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Not listening yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Listening on port %u; no client connected."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Listening on port %u; clients connected: %u."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "What the game sends shows here while it runs (Tab in a game): each name, its type, its meaning and its value. README.md lists them for every game."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "What this game sends"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp src/ui/ui_controls.cpp
msgid "Name"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Type"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Value"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/ui_common.cpp
msgid "Each line goes as \"name = value\" at each change, the drive board's in hexadecimal, the others in decimal; a client that connects gets the game's name first, then every value known. Value: the last one, with the outputs off too."
msgstr ""

